# Website Content Positioning — Rajpal Firah & Vishnu

## Audience
Laypeople searching for a lawyer, not other lawyers.

## Three vocabulary layers (do not conflate)

### Layer 1 — SEO & brand (keep these terms)
Use on: `<title>`, meta description, hero, schema/JSON-LD, Media Room, press copy, footer SEO areas.

**Keep:** criminal lawyer, criminal law, criminal defence, criminal lawyer Malaysia

**Never** replace site-wide with only "litigation specialist."

### Layer 2 — "How We Can Help" section only (layman-facing)
- **Primary (~80%):** Criminal & Civil Litigation — listed first, larger featured cards
  - Card title (layman): "Criminal Law & Defence" / "Civil Disputes & Court Claims"
  - Professional term line: `service-card__term` — "Criminal Litigation" / "Civil Litigation"
  - Body: plain English + court hierarchy (Magistrates', Sessions, High Court, Court of Appeal, Federal Court)
- **Secondary (~20%):** Corporate Advisory, Conveyancing, Probate & Estate Administration — smaller secondary grid

Do not make the homepage feel like a general practice.

### Layer 3 — Professional (profiles, CVs, attorney cards)
Use: Criminal Litigation, Civil Litigation

## Dual-label pattern for service cards
```
<h3> Criminal Law & Defence </h3>   ← layman + SEO-readable
<p class="service-card__term">Criminal Litigation</p>   ← professional
<p class="service-card__desc">…plain English + examples…</p>
```

## What not to do
- Remove "criminal lawyer" from meta/hero/media to sound more premium
- Use only jargon in service cards with no plain-English explanation
- Give equal visual weight to secondary areas as to litigation
